Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth (SIBO)
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Postprandial severe bloating/flatulence, Chronic watery diarrhoea, Positive carbohydrate breath test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-055
Reason for Visit: Agonizing, severe abdominal bloating, immense gas pain, and explosive watery diarrhoea occurring rapidly after every meal, causing severe distress and an inability to eat.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting abdominal distension and high-frequency watery diarrhoea leading to acute distress and suspected dehydration. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to definitively exclude an acute mechanical small bowel obstruction, assess volume status, and provide immediate targeted symptomatic relief prior to standard clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Within 20 minutes of eating anything, my stomach blows up like a balloon, the gas pain is blinding, and I have to run to the toilet."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 4-month history of chronic watery diarrhoea and frequent loose stools. The defining symptom is profound, severe abdominal bloating and visible distension rapidly worsening after meals. Reports severe flatulence, excessive belching, and crampy periumbilical abdominal pain. Occasionally notes steatorrhoea presenting as bulky, foul-smelling, floating stools. Reports mild unintentional weight loss and generalized fatigue. Patient has a known history of underlying intestinal dysmotility following previous abdominal surgery.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but highly anxious; actively avoiding food due to fear of the severe postprandial reaction.
Social/Safety Concerns: Nutritional compromise and mild dehydration due to food avoidance and chronic diarrhoea.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, visibly uncomfortable, and frequently rubbing the distended abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended and entirely tympanic to percussion, indicating massive gaseous accumulation. Active, high-pitched bowel sounds are present. Diffuse, mild periumbilical tenderness is noted on deep palpation, but there is an absolute absence of guarding, rigidity, or localized rebound peritonism. Rectal examination deferred. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with postprandial severe bloating/flatulence and chronic watery diarrhoea; a positive carbohydrate breath test demonstrating early marked elevation in exhaled hydrogen/methane is expected pending formal outpatient testing.
Environmental Observations: Normal living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Has attempted multiple over-the-counter probiotics with worsening of symptoms.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth (SIBO) - Status: Exacerbated (Severe acute postprandial functional distension)
Differentials:

Mechanical Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The ongoing passage of massive amounts of flatus and watery stool, and the soft, tympanitic (gas-filled) rather than rigid abdomen, though the previous surgical history demands careful exclusion.

Coeliac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute, explosive onset of massive gas specifically within 20 minutes of any food, rather than a delayed autoimmune response to gluten specifically, though formal serology remains indicated.

Irritable Bowel Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of steatorrhoea, weight loss, and the specific surgical history causing dysmotility, which heavily favour a structural/microbiome overgrowth pathology rather than a purely functional disorder.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of massive functional gaseous distension and osmotic diarrhoea, directly precipitated by eating. The severity of the cramping and distension in a patient with a surgical history mandated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively exclude a catastrophic mechanical adhesive bowel obstruction. Clinical examination firmly established severe functional gaseous distension (tympanitic, soft abdomen) and definitively excluded peritonism or an absolute mechanical block. Diagnosing this at the bedside prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit. The patient was instructed to cease probiotics (which were actively feeding the overgrowth) and was provided with antispasmodic therapy, safely stabilizing the crisis overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Hyoscine Butylbromide (Buscopan) 20mg immediately, then QID PRN for acute gas spasms. Generated a non-urgent referral for a formal Lactulose Hydrogen/Methane Breath Test and comprehensive bloods (B12, Folate, Iron studies).
Positive Behavioral Change: Completely stop taking all over-the-counter probiotic supplements immediately. In this specific condition, adding millions of new bacteria to an already overgrown small intestine physically provides more organisms to ferment your food, directly worsening the massive gas production and agonizing bloating you are experiencing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss breath test results and initiate targeted, non-absorbable antibiotic therapy (e.g., Rifaximin).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the abdominal pain becomes sudden, continuous, and unbearable, if the abdomen becomes hard like a board, or if uncontrollable bilious vomiting begins, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A specialized breath test is required to definitively measure the exact type and amount of bacterial overgrowth.
Clinical Course: The severe gas spasms will be reduced by the antispasmodic medication, but definitively stopping the reaction requires a specific course of antibiotics to clear the misplaced bacteria.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The physical examination today showed no signs of a completely blocked bowel. However, if severe, sharp pain wakes the patient from a deep sleep, or if the vomiting of green fluid occurs, medical care must be sought immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Normal gut bacteria belong in the large bowel. Due to your previous surgery, these bacteria have migrated backward and overgrown into the small intestine. When you eat, these misplaced bacteria immediately ferment the food before your body can digest it, instantly producing massive volumes of gas (causing the blinding pain and ballooning) and pulling water into the bowel (causing the watery diarrhoea). The prescribed medication will temporarily paralyze the painful muscle spasms.
Actionable Steps: Adopt a strict 'Low FODMAP' diet starting tomorrow morning. This diet eliminates the specific highly-fermentable carbohydrates that these bacteria use as fuel, physically starving them and directly stopping the rapid, massive gas production after meals.

